<details><summary>struck: 2026-09-19T10:50:32.824592Z \u2014 rtica-assessor supersedes the RT-ICA snapshot dated 2026-09-19; assessment pass confirms all conditions are AVAILABLE, no gaps block planning</summary>\\n\\nRT-ICA Reconciliation (orchestrator, pre-planning): The RT-ICA section above contains two entries that disagree on one point \u2014 condition 4 (compact-manifest snapshot) says the over-budget call site \\\"has access to the caller-narrowed signal\\\" (AVAILABLE); the swarm rtica-assessor entry says \\\"_build_over_budget_view() receives no request parameters\\\" (also AVAILABLE, different framing). Both are true at different scopes and this was not previously reconciled: `_build_over_budget_view(result, full_chars, selector)` (server.py:2352, call site server.py:2681) does NOT currently receive `section`/`sections` \u2014 those locals exist only in the enclosing `backlog_view` tool body, not the builder function's own signature. Threading a \\\"caller already narrowed\\\" flag into `_build_over_budget_view` requires a signature change (new parameter), not just reading an existing one. This is now the authoritative statement \u2014 the plan must include this signature change as a task, not assume the signal is already reachable inside the function.\\n\\nSecond finding, more significant, surfaced during orchestrator pre-planning verification (not previously assessed by any swarm agent): `_build_over_budget_view()` is built entirely within `backlog_view`'s legacy PASSTHROUGH fallback code path in server.py (~line 2600-2683), which is architecturally separate from `disclosure_handler.py`'s `navigate=`/`map=`/`head=`/`skip_tokens=` system. The over-budget response's `sections_index` is produced by `_sections_index_from_result()` \u2014 a simple `result.sections` dict enumeration emitting `[N] Name (count)` lines. The `navigate=` mechanism in `disclosure_handler.py` resolves ordinals built by `ordinal_mapper.py`'s `OrdinalPathMapper.build_map()`, which runs a full markdown-parsing pipeline (`ItemContentNormalizer` \u2192 `MarkdownIndexer`/`MarkdownItParser`) entirely disjoint from `_sections_index_from_result()`. These are two independent addressing schemes in the same file. Verified via direct read this session (`grep -n \\\"def _sections_index_from_result\\\"` server.py:2317, `grep -n \\\"def build_map\\\"` ordinal_mapper.py:327, plus reading ordinal_mapper.py's module docstring).\\n\\nConsequence for planning: recommending `navigate=<ordinal>` in `_build_over_budget_view()`'s `_usage` string, using an ordinal drawn from `sections_index`'s `[N]` bracket numbers, is UNVERIFIED to actually resolve via `navigate=` \u2014 those numbers were never confirmed to be valid inputs to `OrdinalPathMapper.resolve()`. If they are not, naming `navigate=` in this hint would itself be a NEW R7 violation (prohibition 3 \u2014 naming a mechanism/address not actually valid in that same response). This is an open design question for the architect phase to resolve with evidence (read `OrdinalPathMapper.resolve()` and `valid_ordinals()` and test against an actual `[N]`-style index), not a settled implementation detail. Three known candidate resolutions, all R7-compliant, to choose between on evidence:\\n1. Give the over-budget response a genuine ordinal (or a full `map=True`-style structural map) so `navigate=` can be named validly.\\n2. Name `map=True` as the next call instead of `navigate=` \u2014 `map=True` needs no address argument and is documented as always under 2,000 tokens, so it sidesteps the addressing-mismatch question entirely.\\n3. Only name `navigate=` conditionally, when a valid ordinal is actually present in the response being returned.\\n\\nDate: 2026-08-21\\nDecision: APPROVED \u2014 condition on the architect phase resolving the ordinal-compatibility open question above before the over-budget-view fix is implemented; the compact-manifest (`_hint`) fix has no such open question and may proceed as scoped.\\n</details>\\n