l \u2014 see Effort section.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Benefits\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-20T21:30:39.038127Z</sub>\\n\\n- Agents converge to the correct narrow query faster instead of defaulting to full-content loads, reducing wasted context-window spend across every `backlog_view()` call.\\n- Agents that hit an over-budget response after narrowing are pointed at a mechanism (`navigate=`/`head=`/`skip_tokens=`) that can actually resolve their state, instead of being told to repeat an action already exhausted \u2014 removing a retry-loop failure mode.\\n- Brings `backlog_core/server.py` into compliance with R7 (`agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md`), completing the operationalization of the normative requirements PR #3056 established, and unblocks parent item #3054.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-19T11:19:24.933217Z</sub>\\n\\n- Agents converge to the correct narrow query faster instead of defaulting to full-content loads, reducing wasted context-window spend across every `backlog_view()` call.\\n- Agents that hit an over-budget response after narrowing are pointed at `map=True` (owner-approved 2026-09-19) instead of being told to repeat an action already exhausted, removing a retry-loop failure mode for the common case.\\n- Brings `backlog_core/server.py` closer to compliance with R7 (`agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md`), completing the `_hint`/reorder and narrowed-branch portions of the requirement PR #3056 established, and unblocks parent item #3054 once merged.\\n- Stating the `map=True` fallback's known limit explicitly (rather than presenting it as unconditionally safe) gives callers and future maintainers an honest signal that full R7 prohibition-3 compliance for this path still depends on #3059 shipping MAP pagination \u2014 preventing a false-done read of this item once PR #3642 lands.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Research\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-20T09:14:32.531697Z</sub>\\n\\n## Wave 0 Technical Research \u2014 hint-construction convention\\n\\n### Established pattern: `next_call`\\n\\nThree call sites in `backlog_core/` and `sam_schema/` build a machine-actionable continuation\\nhint under the literal key `next_call`. All three share the same shape:\\n\\n1. **`backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py` `_handle_extract()` (around line 484-490)** \u2014 the\\n   pattern the item's context already cites as canonical:\\n   ```python\\n   next_call: str | None = None\\n   if bounded.truncated:\\n       next_skip = skip_tokens + bounded.returned_tokens\\n       next_call = (\\n           f'backlog_view(selector=\\\"{selector}\\\", navigate=\\\"{ordinal}\\\", '\\n           f\\\"head={head_tokens}, skip_tokens={next_skip})\\\"\\n       )\\n   ```\\n2. **`backlog_core/server.py` `backlog_list()` (around line 2267-2268)**:\\n   ```python\\n   if has_more:\\n       response[\\\"next_call\\\"] = f\\\"backlog_list(offset={offset + effective_limit}, limit={effective_limit})\\\"\\n   ```\\n3. **`sam_schema/server.py` `_paginate_results()` (around line 175-177)**, a shared helper used\\n   by both `sam_plan` and `sam_task` list/ready actions:\\n   ```python\\n   if has_more:\\n       next_offset = offset + len(page)\\n       result.next_call = f\\\"{tool_name}(offset={next_offset}, limit={effective_limit})\\\"\\n   ```\\n\\nCommon shape across all three (this is the convention a fix must match):\\n\\n- **Field name**: `next_call`, always a bare string (or `None`/absent), never nested under a\\n  prose paragraph.\\n- **Conditional, not unconditional**: the field is populated only when a next step is genuinely\\n  actionable (`bounded.truncated`, `has_more`). When there is nothing left to fetch, the field is\\n  omitted/`None` \u2014 the hint never recommends an action the caller has already exhausted (this is\\n  literally R7's second clause).\\n- **Content is a literal, copy-pasteable call expression**: `tool_name(param=value, ...)`, not a\\n  sentence describing what to do. The reader can paste it directly.\\n- **Parameters cite exactly the current response's state**: the *cumulative* position is computed\\n  from values already in scope at the call site \u2014 `skip_tokens + bounded.returned_tokens`,\\n  `offset + effective_limit`, `offset + len(page)` \u2014 not a static default. The caller never has to\\n  compute the next offset itself.\\n- **One suggestion, not several**: each of the three sites emits exactly one `next_call` string,\\n  matching R7's \\\"never recommend retrieving everything as its first suggestion\\\" \u2014 there is no\\n  competing alternative offered.\\n\\n### The two violating call sites do not follow this convention\\n\\n`_build_compact_manifest()` (`backlog_core/server.py` around 2275-2314) and\\n`_build_over_budget_view()` (around 2352-2392) both diverge from every element of the pattern\\nabove:\\n\\n- Different field names (`_hint`, `_usage`) instead of `next_call`.\\n- Content is prose containing two-to-three alternative calls as literal strings embedded in a\\n  multi-line human-readable message, not a single machine-actionable expression.\\n- Unconditional: both functions always populate the field on every call, regardless of what the\\n  caller has already requested. `_build_compact_manifest`'s `_hint` recommends \\\"Load full content\\\"\\n  as its *first* line unconditionally \u2014 this is the literal violation of R7's first clause (\\\"must\\n  never recommend retrieving everything as its first suggestion\\\").\\n- No use of already-known caller state: at the `_build_over_budget_view` call site\\n  (`server.py` around line 2681, inside the `if not summary:` branch), `sections_filter` (set at\\n  line 2633 from the `sections` param) and `section` (the raw param) are both in scope and\\n  non-None whenever the caller already narrowed the request \u2014 but neither is passed into\\n  `_build_over_budget_view`, so the returned `_usage` string always suggests `sections=`/`section=`\\n  usage even to a caller who is already using it. This is the literal violation of R7's second\\n  clause (\\\"must never recommend an action the caller has already exhausted\\\").\\n\\n### R7 normative text (`docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md`, \\\"R7 \u2014 Hints must be\\nactionable\\\")\\n\\n> A response that withholds content states what the caller must do to obtain it, using addresses\\n> valid in that same response. A hint must never recommend retrieving everything as its first\\n> suggestion, never recommend an action the caller has already exhausted, and never name a\\n> mechanism absent from the response it accompanies.\\n\\nR6 (same doc) explicitly cross-references R7's hint as the mechanism for reaching paginated\\ncontent: \\\"the caller reaches the remainder with a same-tool page request named by R7's hint.\\\"\\n\\n### Prior art: no ADR governs hint *string construction* specifically\\n\\nNo ADR or PR title matching \\\"hint\\\" was found scoped to the construction convention itself \u2014 R7 in\\nthe consumption contract doc is the only normative spec, and the `next_call` pattern above is\\nimplicit convention (established by the three working call sites and their tests), not\\ndocumented separately. The fix should express its `next_call` construction convention inline in\\ncode (docstring/comment), consistent with how `_handle_extract()` already documents its own\\n`next_call` semantics.\\n\\n### Existing test coverage (prior art for what a fix's tests must add)\\n\\n`tests/test_backlog_core_server.py` already asserts *fidelity* (the hint embeds the caller's exact\\nselector) for both violating sites, but not R7 compliance:\\n\\n- `test_backlog_view_summary_true_hint_contains_selector` (around line 1046) \u2014 asserts `_hint`\\n  contains the exact selector string, nothing about suggestion ordering.\\n- `test_build_over_budget_view_usage_mentions_sections_param` (around line 3898) \u2014 asserts `_usage`\\n  contains `sections=` and the selector, nothing about conditioning on prior narrowing.\\n\\nNeither existing test would catch the R7 violations described above (first-suggestion ordering,\\nalready-exhausted advice) \u2014 a fix needs new tests asserting: (1) `_hint`'s first suggestion is\\nsection-scoped when `sections_index` is present, not full-content load, and (2) `_usage`/an\\nequivalent field omits or reorders the sections-narrowing suggestion when the caller already\\nsupplied `section`/`sections`.\\n\\n### Recurrence assessment\\n\\nConfirmed two call sites need the fix (`_build_compact_manifest`, `_build_over_budget_view`); no\\nthird violating site exists \u2014 every other hint-construction call site in `backlog_core/` and\\n`sam_schema/` (`backlog_list`, `_handle_extract`, `_paginate_results`) already follows the\\n`next_call` convention correctly.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-19T09:41:52.267979Z</sub>\\n\\n# Research refresh \u2014 backlog #3060 \u2014 2026-09-19\\n\\nAll file:line citations below are against `origin/main` (HEAD `a75f11f0a5892979c5d72c316a6d3fe5a1007938`,\\nverified equal to `origin/main` via `git rev-parse HEAD` / `git rev-parse origin/main` at research time).\\nPR #3642 (branch `fix/3060-backlog-view-r7-hints`) is OPEN, not merged \u2014 its changes are NOT in the\\ntext quoted below; they are reported separately, sourced from the PR body/commits via `gh pr view`.\\n\\n## 1. Current `_hint` text (compact manifest, `summary=True` path)\\n\\nSOURCE: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:2101-2104` (accessed 2026-09-19, origin/main)\\n\\n```python\\n\\\"_hint\\\": (\\n    f\\\"Load full content: backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False)\\\\n\\\"\\n    f\\\"Load specific sections: backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False, section='<index, title, or /regex/>')\\\"\\n),\\n```\\n\\nUnconditional, two-line prose string. First line recommends the unscoped full-content load\\n(`summary=False`, no `section=`) before the narrower section-scoped option on the second line.\\nSame shape as when this item's Research/Fact-Check section quoted it (2026-08-21) \u2014 no drift.\\n\\n## 2. Current `_usage` text (over-budget path, `summary=False` and payload > budget)\\n\\nSOURCE: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:2147-2189` (`_build_over_budget_view`),\\n`_usage` field at lines 2176-2184 (accessed 2026-09-19, origin/main). Budget constant:\\n`_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET = 4_000` at line 231.\\n\\n```python\\n\\\"_usage\\\": (\\n    f\\\"This response exceeded the {_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET}-token budget \\\"\\n    f\\\"({full_chars} chars in full form). \\\"\\n    \\\"Use the sections_index below to identify which sections you need, \\\"\\n    \\\"then request them individually:\\\\n\\\"\\n    f\\\"  backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False, sections=['Section Name'])\\\\n\\\"\\n    f\\\"  backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False, section='0,1,3')\\\\n\\\"\\n    f\\\"  backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False, section='/regex/')\\\"\\n),\\n```\\n\\nUnconditional \u2014 built with no branch on whether the caller already passed `section=`/`sections=`.\\nThe function signature is `_build_over_budget_view(result, full_chars, selector)` (line 2147) \u2014 it\\nreceives no parameter carrying \\\"caller already narrowed\\\" state. `map=`, `navigate=`, `head=`, and\\n`skip_tokens=` do not appear anywhere in this string on origin/main.\\n\\n## 3. Fields an over-budget response carries\\n\\nSOURCE: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:2165-2189` (accessed 2026-09-19, origin/main)\\n\\n`_build_over_budget_view()` returns: `number`, `title`, `priority`, `status`, `description`,\\n`section_filter_miss`, `status_source`, `unavailable_capabilities`, `_over_budget`, `_full_chars`,\\n`_usage`, and \u2014 conditionally, when `_sections_index_from_result()` returns non-empty \u2014\\n`sections_index`.\\n\\n## 4. Does any of those fields give an ordinal `navigate=` resolves?\\n\\nNo. `sections_index` (when present) is built by `_sections_index_from_result()`\\n(`plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:2112-2144`), which emits lines of the form\\n`\\\"[{idx}] {name} ({count} entries)\\\"` \u2014 a bracket-numbered index over `enumerate(result.sections)`.\\n\\nThis is a different addressing scheme from the one `navigate=` resolves. Per\\n`plugins/development-harness/docs/component-architecture.md:44-48` (accessed 2026-09-19):\\n\\n> Two numbering schemes exist today and they are not the same thing. The engine's dot-path\\n> addresses reach sections, sub-headings, and code fences at any depth; they are the surviving\\n> scheme. The still-shipped bracket-numbered index emitted by the hand-built section directory\\n> addresses only top-level sections and does not paginate; #3057 tracks removing it with the code\\n> that produces it.\\n\\nThe dot-path ordinal grammar `navigate=` actually accepts is `^\\\\d+(\\\\.\\\\d+)*(\\\\.code\\\\.\\\\d+)?$`\\n(SOURCE: `plugins/development-harness/docs/mcp-progressive-disclosure-contract.md:29-36`, e.g.\\n`\\\"4.0\\\"`, `\\\"4.0.1\\\"`, `\\\"4.0.code.0\\\"`) \u2014 produced by `OrdinalPathMapper` in\\n`progressive_markdown/ordinal_mapper.py`, an entirely separate code path from\\n`_sections_index_from_result()`. No test or source in `backlog_core/server.py` confirms the\\nbracket `[N]` values are valid `navigate=` inputs; this item's own RT-ICA reconciliation entry\\n(2026-08-20T21:50:17Z, `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md:238-245`) flagged this as unverified and\\nnamed it a risk: naming `navigate=` with a bracket-derived number would itself be a new R7\\nviolation if the numbers don't resolve.\\n\\n**Conclusion: no field in the over-budget response currently gives an ordinal `navigate=`\\nresolves.** The response has no dot-path-addressable content at all.\\n\\n## 5. R7 text and its prohibitions\\n\\nSOURCE: `plugins/development-harness/docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md:158-163`\\n(accessed 2026-09-19)\\n\\n```text\\n### R7 \u2014 Hints must be actionable\\n\\nA response that withholds content states what the caller must do to obtain it, using\\naddresses valid in that same response. A hint must never recommend retrieving everything as\\nits first suggestion, never recommend an action the caller has already exhausted, and never\\nname a mechanism absent from the response it accompanies.\\n```\\n\\nThree prohibitions, in order:\\n1. Never recommend retrieving everything as the first suggestion.\\n2. Never recommend an action the caller has already exhausted.\\n3. Never name a mechanism absent from the response it accompanies.\\n\\nAgainst origin/main's current text (Sections 1-2 above): `_hint` violates prohibition 1 (leads\\nwith `summary=False`, the unscoped full load). `_usage` violates prohibition 2 (unconditionally\\nsuggests `section=`/`sections=` narrowing even to a caller who already narrowed and is still over\\nbudget) and, per Section 4 above, would also violate prohibition 3 if it named `navigate=`\\nwithout a resolvable address in the response \u2014 which is exactly why it currently names none of\\n`map=`, `navigate=`, `head=`, `skip_tokens=` at all.\\n\\n## 6. State of #3057 and #3059\\n\\n**#3057** \u2014 OPEN. Title: \\\"refactor: route the default item-read path through the markdown\\nengine.\\\" SOURCE: `gh issue view 3057` (accessed 2026-09-19). Acceptance criteria include \\\"The\\nbracket-numbered index is gone; addresses returned are the engine's dot-path scheme\\\" and \\\"The\\nthree duplicate index builders are deleted, not left unreferenced.\\\" Parent: #3054. Until #3057\\nlands, `_sections_index_from_result()`'s bracket `[N]` output (Section 4 above) remains the only\\nsection directory the over-budget/compact-manifest responses can offer.\\n\\n**#3059** \u2014 OPEN. Title: \\\"refactor: replace reimplemented navigation with the engine's navigation\\nlayer.\\\" SOURCE: `gh issue view 3059` (accessed 2026-09-19). Its body states, with its own verified\\nevidence: \\\"The map handler in `disclosure_handler.py` assembles its output as\\n`map_text = \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\".join(mapper.format_map_line(e) for e in entries)` ... No budget check, no\\ntruncation, no pagination.\\\" Confirmed independently in\\n`plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md:1278-1286` (\\\"Resolution Index vs Map\\nText Split (\u00a75.3)\\\", accessed 2026-09-19):\\n\\n> `MapResponse.map_text` contains every formatted entry returned by `build_map()`. It is not\\n> bounded or paginated and does not omit entries. `MapResponse.over_budget` compares the\\n> represented level-1 content estimate with `TOKEN_BUDGET`; it is diagnostic only. #3059 tracks\\n> paginating MAP without dropping any address, and #3062 tracks content-identity follow-up\\n> requests.\\n\\n`backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md:1219` also lists #3059 (with #2969) against \\\"MAP/NAVIGATE/EXTRACT\\nserving,\\\" noting \\\"MAP is complete but unpaginated.\\\" So **MAP output currently has no size bound**\\n\u2014 a `map=True` hint can itself return an over-the-harness-cap response on a large item until\\n#3059 ships.\\n\\n## 7. PR #3642 (open, not merged) \u2014 what it changes, and what it still leaves unmet\\n\\nSOURCE: `gh pr view 3642` (accessed 2026-09-19). Base `main`, head\\n`fix/3060-backlog-view-r7-hints`, state OPEN, `mergeable: MERGEABLE`, not a draft. Body states\\n\\\"Refs #3060 (partial: this PR does not close the issue).\\\"\\n\\nChanges (`backlog_core/server.py` only, per PR body):\\n- `_hint` reordered: section-narrowing call listed first, full-content load second.\\n- `_build_over_budget_view()` gains a keyword-only `narrowed_to_single_section: bool = False`\\n  flag. `backlog_view` sets it when the caller passed `section=`/`sections=` and exactly one\\n  section remains in `full_response[\\\"sections\\\"]`. When set, `_usage` names\\n  `backlog_view(selector=..., map=True)` instead of repeating section-narrowing advice.\\n- No `navigate=<ordinal>` is named anywhere, per the PR body's own reasoning: \\\"The `[N]` numbers\\n  in `sections_index` come from enumerating a dict, so they can differ from the `OrdinalPathMapper`\\n  ordinals when two sections share a title\\\" \u2014 consistent with Section 4 above.\\n\\nPR body's own self-assessment table against #3060's acceptance criteria:\\n\\n| #3060 criterion | Status after PR #3642 |\\n|---|---|\\n| No hint recommends retrieving everything first | Met for `_hint`. The `summary` parameter's `Field(description=...)` still reads \\\"`_hint` showing how to load full content or specific sections\\\" (full content first) \u2014 not changed by this PR. |\\n| The over-budget hint offers an action a narrowed caller has not taken | Met: `_usage` names `map=True` when `narrowed_to_single_section` is set. |\\n| Hints name only mechanisms present in the response | **Not met** for the narrowed `_usage`: `map=`, `navigate=`, `head=`, `skip_tokens=` still do not appear as fields in the over-budget response body itself \u2014 the hint names `map=True` as a call to make, which R7 permits (a hint may recommend a call not yet made), but the response carries no `map=`-derived content field to point back into. |\\n| Addresses in a hint are valid in that response | The narrowed `_usage` names no address (no `navigate=<ordinal>`), so this criterion doesn't apply to it. |\\n\\n## 8. Repo owner decision, 2026-09-19 (comment on #3060)\\n\\nSOURCE: `gh issue view 3060 --json comments` (accessed 2026-09-19), comment by `Jamie-BitFlight`,\\n`createdAt: 2026-09-19T06:59:44Z`, verbatim:\\n\\n> **Decision (repo owner, 2026-09-19):** keep `map=True` as the narrowed over-budget hint, and\\n> record its limit. PR #3642 references this issue and does not close it.\\n\\nThe same comment enumerates what stays open on #3060 after PR #3642:\\n1. The narrowed hint can lead to an over-budget response \u2014 grooming had accepted `map=True`\\n   because it \\\"is documented as always under 2,000 tokens\\\"; #3059 shows that's false (Section 6\\n   above). Safe for every item size only once #3059 paginates MAP.\\n2. R7's third prohibition is not met for the narrowed hint: `map=`, `navigate=`, `head=`,\\n   `skip_tokens=` do not appear in the over-budget response (Section 3-4 above).\\n3. The `summary` parameter's `Field(description=...)` still reads full-content-first (Section 7\\n   table, row 1).\\n4. Acceptance criteria 2 and 4 on #3060 name mechanisms/test functions rather than observable\\n   checks, per `groom/swarm.md` \u2014 need re-grooming.\\n5. Plan `Pe6bcc5c2` reports 100% complete but its implementation was never merged (lived only on\\n   unpushed local branch `worktree-agent-a07620dae7e4b301c`); PR #3642 re-applies that work onto\\n   current main.\\n\\nA separate, not-yet-filed finding from the same comment: five agent files call\\n`backlog_view(summary=False)` directly \u2014 `alignment-analyst`, `backlog-item-groomer`,\\n`classifier`, `impact-analyst`, `rtica-assessor`.\\n\\n## Gaps / not verified\\n\\n- Whether PR #3642's test suite run (1151 tests, prek clean, per the PR body) has been\\n  independently re-confirmed by this research pass \u2014 not run here; reported as PR-author claim\\n  only, sourced to the PR body text.\\n- Whether `map=True`'s \\\"record its limit\\\" instruction (owner decision, item 1 above) has been\\n  acted on anywhere yet \u2014 not found in any file read during this pass; treat as open per the\\n  decision comment itself.\\n- No live check was made of `groom/swarm.md`'s acceptance-criteria wording rule beyond the\\n  citation already present in the 2026-09-19 owner comment.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-20T21:21:51.090823Z</sub>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\nAlignment assessment: **ALIGNED**\\n\\n### Concerns\\n\\n| Category | Proposed Change Excerpt | Mission Source Violated | Citation |\\n|----------|------------------------|-------------------------|----------|\\n| \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 |\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\n0 concerns identified \u2014 proposed change is consistent with product mission and historical direction.\\n\\n**Rationale**: The fix updates runtime hint strings in `backlog_core/server.py` (`_build_compact_manifest()` and `_build_over_budget_view()`) to comply with R7 (\\\"Hints must be actionable\\\") from the **Agent Markdown Consumption \u2014 Behaviour Specification** (`docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md`:159-164). This requirement was established as normative via PR #3056 (merged 2026-08-20), which added the component architecture map and expanded the markdown consumption contract with design decisions R1\u2013R8.\\n\\n**Mission alignment**: The development-harness plugin's stated purpose is to \\\"orchestrate feature development through a structured 7-stage pipeline\\\" with agents as the primary consumer. Ensuring that agent-facing runtime hints comply with normative markdown-consumption requirements is core to that mission \u2014 hints are read \\\"on every call, at the moment of the decision\\\" (backlog item description) and directly steer agent behavior.\\n\\n**Design principle alignment**: The contract (`agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md:1-3`) explicitly states \\\"Every consumer of every operation described here is an AI agent\\\" and R7 mandates that hints \\\"never recommend retrieving everything as its first suggestion, never recommend an action the caller has already exhausted, and never name a mechanism absent from the response it accompanies.\\\" This fix ensures `backlog_core` follows the architectural requirement that agent-facing behavior comply with the markdown consumption contract. Component architecture (`component-architecture.md`) assigns `backlog_core` responsibility to consume and respect normative requirements from the markdown contract.\\n\\n**Historical direction**: PR #3056 (merged 2026-08-20) closed \\\"gaps a six-reviewer pass found in the markdown consumption contract\\\" and explicitly recorded R6\u2013R8 as normative. This fix (#3060) operationalizes one of those requirements (R7) in the live codebase.\\n\\n**Reference implementation**: The fix follows the established pattern from `disclosure_handler.py` (lines 487-490), which correctly constructs hints that reference mechanisms present in the response structure.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-19T10:53:58.654571Z</sub>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\nAlignment assessment: **MISSION_DIVERGENT**\\n\\n### Concerns\\n\\n| Category | Proposed Change Excerpt | Mission Source Violated | Citation |\\n|----------|------------------------|-------------------------|----------|\\n| violates-design-principle | Narrowed `_usage` hint recommends `map=True` when over-budget, per owner's 2026-09-19 decision; `_build_map()` produces unbounded responses with no size cap | R7 (Agent Markdown Consumption Contract): hints must steer toward reliably actionable continuations; map=True recommendation can itself exceed harness cap for large items, recreating R7 prohibition-2 \\\"advice exhausted\\\" one level downstream | `plugins/development-harness/docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md:158-163` (R7, normative per PR #3056 merged 2026-08-20); `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` lines 2147-2189 (`_build_over_budget_view()` `_usage`); #3059 (open, tracks bounding MAP); Impact Radius 7.3 \\\"MAP producer\\\" row (HIGH risk) |\\n| violates-design-principle | PR #3642 (unmerged) removes #3060 from `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` \\\"remaining ownership\\\" table (line ~1220), asserting item is resolved | Architecture contract: governing specifications must accurately reflect current ownership state and completion; table contradicts owner's 2026-09-19 comment listing 4 open sub-items | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md:~1220` (remaining ownership table, unmerged edit); owner's 2026-09-19 comment (4 open items remain: #3057 bracket index removal, #3057 addressing gap, Field description update, +1 more) |\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\n2 concerns identified \u2014 2 violates-design-principle\\n\\n**Design principle violation 1 (R7 implicit principle):** The owner's 2026-09-19 decision to recommend `map=True` as the narrowed over-budget hint is technically R7-compliant at the immediate level (the hint string names a mechanism present in the response). However, the consequence violates R7's underlying design principle: because `_build_map()` has no size cap (issue #3059, open), following the narrowed hint's recommendation can itself exceed the harness cap for large items, leaving the agent with no further named next steps \u2014 which is the same \\\"advice exhausted\\\" failure that R7 prohibition 2 (never recommend an exhausted action) targets, one level of indirection downstream. Development-harness mission is to \\\"orchestrate feature development... with agents as the primary consumer\\\" (AGENTS.md); if hints recommend operations that aren't reliably actionable for the items agents work on (large items), the orchestration is not reliable.\\n\\n**Design principle violation 2 (documentation contract):** PR #3642 (unmerged, part of this fix) removes #3060 from the \\\"remaining ownership\\\" table in `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md`, asserting the issue is no longer open work. This contradicts the owner's own 2026-09-19 comment, which lists 4 named items still open on #3060 after PR #3642 merges (bracket `[N]` index removal tracked by #3057, addressing gap tracked by #3057, Field description update, and one additional item). A governing specification that asserts completion when 4 sub-items remain open is a contract violation.\\n\\n**Owner awareness:** Both concerns are known to the owner and represent conscious trade-off decisions. The owner's 2026-09-19 comment explicitly approves keeping `map=True` \\\"and record its limit,\\\" acknowledging that #3059 (bounding MAP) is a required follow-up. The observations note the owner has also approved dropping the ARCHITECTURE.md edit from PR #3642, which mitigates concern 2.\\n\\n**Reference implementation:** The fix follows the pattern established by `disclosure_handler.py` (lines 487-490) for constructing compliant hints. The immediate reorder of `_hint` in `_build_compact_manifest()` and the `narrowed_to_single_section` branch in `_build_over_budget_view()` correctly address R7 for the immediate response. The divergence is specific to the owner's decision on what the narrowed hint should recommend when the immediate response is too large.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-19T10:58:45.895172Z</sub>\\n\\nSupersedes entry 2026-09-19T10:53:58.654571Z\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\nAlignment assessment: **MISSION_DIVERGENT**\\n\\n### Concerns\\n\\n| Category | Proposed Change Excerpt | Mission Source Violated | Citation |\\n|----------|------------------------|-------------------------|----------|\\n| violates-design-principle | Narrowed `_usage` hint recommends `map=True` when over-budget; R7 prohibition 3 violation: `map=` parameter is not a field in the over-budget response | R7 (Agent Markdown Consumption Contract):159-163: \\\"never name a mechanism absent from the response it accompanies\\\" \u2014 the over-budget response (lines 2165-2189) contains selector, status, description, sections_index, and _usage fields only; `map=`, `navigate=`, `head=`, and `skip_tokens=` parameters are not present as response fields | `plugins/development-harness/docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md:158-163` (R7, normative per PR #3056 merged 2026-08-20); `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` lines 2165-2189 (`_build_over_budget_view()` response fields); owner's comment item 2 |\\n| violates-design-principle | PR #3642 (unmerged) removes #3060 from `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` \\\"Consumer guidance and contract identity\\\" row (line 1223), asserting item is no longer open work | Architecture contract: governing specifications must accurately reflect current ownership state and completion. Owner's comment lists